## 3. Data facts (verified 2026-10-17 from OpenML)

- OpenML 61 `iris`, version 1, ARFF, `default_target_attribute = class`, licence Public,
  URL `https://openml.org/data/v1/download/61/iris.arff`, **md5 `ad484452702105cbf3d30f8deaba39a9`** (I downloaded it and the md5 matches).
- 150 instances, 4 numeric features (sepal length/width, petal length/width, cm), 3 classes × 50 (setosa, versicolor, virginica),
  no missing values. Setosa is linearly separable from the others; versicolor/virginica overlap.
- This copy carries the UCI transcription differences from Fisher (1936) noted by Bezdek et al. (1999): data row 35 is
  `4.9,3.1,1.5,0.1` (Fisher: petal width 0.2) and row 38 is `4.9,3.1,1.5,0.1` (Fisher: `4.9,3.6,1.4,0.1`). The objective says to use
  OpenML 61, so **use it as-is**; a one-line note in the paper is optional.
- The data is ordered by class, so shuffled stratified splitting is required (it is what the objective asks).

## 4. Expectations and conventions the study should follow

**Expected magnitude (planning estimate, not a result):** both methods are known to reach roughly 0.95–0.97 accuracy on Iris; the
difference is expected to be small and its interval to include 0. A null difference is a legitimate result and should be reported as
such, not tuned away. Each outer test fold has 30 samples, so per-fold accuracies are multiples of 1/30 and many paired differences
will be exactly 0 — the figure and interval method should cope with that.

**Pipeline conventions**
- Standardise features inside each training fold (Pipeline), fitted on training data only — essential for k-NN distances; apply the
  same preprocessing to both methods.
- Logistic regression: multinomial softmax (in scikit-learn ≥1.5 the lbfgs default is multinomial for >2 classes; `multi_class` is
  deprecated/removed, so state "multinomial" explicitly in the paper rather than relying on the argument). The objective does not ask to
  tune LR; keep a fixed, stated C (default 1.0) and say so. Tuning only k is what the objective specifies.
- k-NN: k selected by **inner** stratified CV within each outer training set (nested CV; Varma & Simon 2006 — selecting k on the
  outer folds would bias the estimate). State the k grid (e.g. 1–30 or odd 1–29), inner folds, tie rule, and report the chosen k's
  distribution across the 50 outer folds.
- Same outer splits for both methods (a fixed `random_state` on `RepeatedStratifiedKFold(n_splits=5, n_repeats=10)`), so the
  difference is paired per fold.

**Interval conventions (the decision belongs to experiment-repo; this is the literature it should compare against)**
- The 50 fold accuracies of repeated CV are not independent (overlapping training sets); a naive t interval over 50 folds is too narrow
  (Dietterich 1998; Nadeau & Bengio 2003; Bouckaert & Frank 2004; Bates, Hastie & Tibshirani 2024).
- Standard choice for repeated k-fold: the **corrected resampled t** interval (Nadeau & Bengio 2003), as applied to repeated CV by
  Bouckaert & Frank 2004: mean ± t_{0.975, J−1} · sqrt((1/J + n_test/n_train) · s²), with J = 50, n_test/n_train = 30/120 = 0.25,
  s² the sample variance of the 50 fold values (accuracies or paired differences). Use it for both per-method means and the paired
  difference, and name the method in the table caption.
- Any alternative (t over the 10 repetition means, bootstrap, Bates et al. nested-CV interval) is acceptable if named and justified;
  the paper must say which interval is used.

**Reproducibility convention (C1–C4)**
- Same code, same seeds, same data file (checksum) in both runs; record Python and library versions (numpy, scipy, scikit-learn) and
  thread settings on both sides; save per-fold accuracies (and chosen k) to files from each run and compare them value by value
  (exact equality, and the max absolute difference if not identical). Report the Ibex node name from inside the job, and the job id.
- "Identical numbers" should be answered for the per-fold results and the reported summaries; if they differ, report by how much and
  the likely cause (library/BLAS versions), not hide it.

**Paper conventions**
- NeurIPS 2026 template in submission (anonymous, default) mode, ≤ 2 pages, compiled with pdflatex; one table (method, mean
  accuracy, 95% CI; paired difference, 95% CI; possibly chosen-k summary) and one figure (e.g. per-fold paired differences or
  per-repetition accuracies for both methods, with the intervals); the figure and table carry the claim, every number traceable to
  code in `report/`.

